module bindings.java.unguardedHostnameVerifierWiring;

// Apache HttpClient wires hostname verification through an X509HostnameVerifier
// handed to the SSL socket factory's constructor. The verifier is optional
// everywhere else in the library -- HttpClientBuilder exposes it only through
// `setHostnameVerifier`, and leaves the field null until a caller sets it --
// while the 4.3 constructor a null verifier was handed to stored it verbatim
// and ran its verify path only when the field was non-null. A builder or
// helper that threads its possibly-null verifier straight into that
// constructor therefore wires a connection factory whose hostname
// verification is silently off for every caller that never configured one.
// That is the same finding as the explicit opt-outs beside it in this family
// (`setHostnameVerifier(true)`, `NoopHostnameVerifier`): hostname verification
// not performed, discovered at the wiring site rather than at the switch.
//
// Three facts, all read off the wiring function's own body. The hand-off: a
// `hostnameVerifier` reaching an `SSLConnectionSocketFactory` or the
// deprecated `SSLSocketFactory` constructor, read off the call-argument pair,
// which keeps the needle on the argument the constructor actually receives --
// same-named getters, `TestX509HostnameVerifier` class references and other
// bare mentions of the word elsewhere in the function do not pair with the
// constructor and do not satisfy it. The null guard: no `hostnameVerifier ==
// null` / `!= null` comparison in the function, the shape of every fix of
// this weakness, which defaults the verifier before the hand-off. And the
// provenance exclusions, each a way the verifier is known non-null: a call
// naming a hostname verifier in the function -- a constructed
// `BrowserCompatHostnameVerifier`, `DefaultHostnameVerifier`, or any project's
// own `*HostnameVerifier` implementation, whose construction is the last
// statement before the hand-off in the library's own documented example -- and
// a `hostnameVerifier` parameter, the pass-through helper whose null-ness is
// its caller's to settle. JDK `javax.net.ssl.HostnameVerifier` arguments ride
// the same needles: the constructor spellings that take them belong to the
// same library and treat null the same way.
//
// Residual, both directions. A verifier defaulted at its field declaration or
// through `Objects.requireNonNull` in a helper the wiring function does not
// name is invisible here, so a provably non-null verifier wired without a
// local guard still reports -- the false-positive direction. And a function
// that both wires an unguarded verifier and makes any other
// hostname-verifier-named call is silenced by the provenance clause -- the
// false-negative direction only.

binding javaNullableHostnameVerifierWiredUnguarded {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and containsAny(node.context.callArg, ["SSLConnectionSocketFactory:hostnameVerifier", "SSLSocketFactory:hostnameVerifier"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.expr, ["hostnameVerifier==null", "hostnameVerifier!=null"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.call, ["HostnameVerifier"])
    and not containsAny(node.context.paramName, ["hostnameVerifier"])
  emit issue code.CertValidationDisabled at node
}
